Description: antinomie: boot the guest its boot age ago and step to the present at the first launch
 A freshly started guest shows minutes of uptime; a machine in use has been
 up for hours (a desktop or laptop) or days to months (a server). The host
 passes the VM's boot age, chosen once per VM, and the clock model of its
 machine as antinomie_boot_age=SECONDS:MODEL; like the other antinomie_*
 options it is taken out of the command line before the kernel prints it.
 .
 The guest boots that long in the past and reaches the present when the
 host first launches a program (the first EXEC command), after the boot's
 own records are written:
 .
 - While booting, the wall clock runs the age behind the time it was given:
   timekeeping_init() sets it back, and do_settimeofday64() shifts every
   setting made before the first launch (the RTC at boot, timesyncd's saved
   time, hwclock). The utmp/wtmp boot record, the journal's first entries
   and the files the boot writes carry that time; the boot time, uptime,
   CLOCK_BOOTTIME, init's start time and dmesg -T agree with them.
 - At the first launch ant_timekeeping_present() steps the wall clock to the
   true time (the true wall time minus CLOCK_MONOTONIC at timekeeping_init()
   plus CLOCK_MONOTONIC now, so a clock set while booting is not counted
   twice) and CLOCK_BOOTTIME forward by the age, as a resume from sleep does,
   then clock_was_set().
 - suspend (desktop and laptop profiles): nothing more; CLOCK_MONOTONIC and
   the kernel log's timestamps do not count the sleep, as after a real one.
 - continuous (server and cloud profiles): from then on programs'
   CLOCK_MONOTONIC runs the age ahead of the kernel's, so it equals
   CLOCK_BOOTTIME: clock_gettime() (timens_add_monotonic()), the vDSO
   (update_vsyscall()), absolute timers, futexes, clock_nanosleep and timerfd
   (timens_ktime_to_host()), the kernel log's timestamps (printk), and each
   CPU's idle time (most of the age, /proc/stat and /proc/uptime).
 .
 The kernel's own CLOCK_MONOTONIC, jiffies and scheduler clock never move:
 the recorder's timestamps, the host's accounting of guest time and every
 kernel timeout run on them. Timestamps the kernel hands out on that clock
 (input events, DRM vblanks) and io_uring's absolute timeouts stay on it.
 .
 Hooked kernel files: kernel/time/timekeeping.c (timekeeping_init(),
 do_settimeofday64(), the step), kernel/time/vsyscall.c,
 include/linux/time_namespace.h, kernel/printk/printk.c, fs/proc/stat.c;
 kernel/antinomie/control.c calls the step before the first launch.
 For a stock-profile kernel's ABI (Debian's BTF, traceable functions and
 symbol versions): ant_timekeeping_present() is notrace, tk_xtime_add() is
 __always_inline as Debian's build inlines it, and printk.c reads the offset
 through <linux/antinomie_stealth.h> (time_namespace.h there changed the
 console exports' symbol versions).
 .
 Kconfig: ANTINOMIE_BOOT_AGE; the image builders list "uptime" in a base's
 stealth support from it.
 .
 Contract revision 21 (a boot option, like antinomie_ring_kib=).
Author: MikiMiku <jonathanirvinjo@gmail.com>
Origin: project
Forwarded: no
Last-Update: 2026-11-05

